// Deliberately faulting, so that the fault path is tested rather than assumed.
//
// The boot stub identity-maps the first four gigabytes and nothing above them,
// so a read at 5 GiB has no page behind it and the CPU raises a page fault.
//
// The address has moved twice, and both times because the read quietly
// succeeded rather than faulting: zero is inside the map, and so was
// 0x50000000 once the map grew to cover the framebuffer. A test that asserts
// on the fault is what noticed; a comment claiming the address was unmapped
// would not have.
try_fault() {
  print("*** reading an address with no page behind it");
  peek64(0x140000000);
} return void;

// The end of the run. The fault demonstration is last, because the fault
// handler stops the machine and anything after it would never happen.
last_lines() {
  gfx_demo();
  con_demo();
  end_lines();
} return void;

// The boot demonstration ends at a prompt rather than at a spin: `sh_run` is
// what `hold` was a placeholder for, and it never returns, so the `poweroff`
// below it is reached only if that ever stops being true.
end_lines() {
  sh_run();
  poweroff();
} return void;
